---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import Evidence from '../../components/Evidence.astro';
import { essays } from '../../data/essays';
import { gh, u } from '../../lib/url';

const essay = essays.find((e) => e.slug === 'release-record')!;

const docs = u('docs/tower/release-records/');
const spec = u('library/tower-release-record-lifecycle/');

// The two files a release leaves in git, trimmed to their shape. The app and its release are Skyport's.
const draft = `# glidepath/releases/next.yaml, in the app repo
owner: priya-k
summary: The gate board shows each gate's change history, and the departure board stops mixing time zones.
notes:
  added:
    - Gate change history endpoint (#189)
  fixed:
    - Departure board times in the airport's own time zone (#190)
risk:
  level: low
  rollback: "Safe to roll back to 1.8.0: no schema change."
targetDate: "2026-10-29"`;
const record = `# releases/flight-api@1.8.0-4f9c2ab.yaml, in gitops-flight-api
schemaVersion: 2
appName: flight-api
imageTag: 1.8.0-4f9c2ab
release:      # the draft above, the mode it shipped under, and the sign-off
guardrails:   # every release gate's result, frozen when the record was sealed
testEvidence: # the check ride the qa gate read, and whether it verified
changeRequest:
humanContext: # after release: how it was checked, follow-ups
outcome:      # added by a second pull request, once it is final`;

// Stills from Tower running against the demo harness in tools/tower-demo: flight-api's invented history.
const still = (f: string) => u(`media/release-record/${f}`);

const evidence = [
  { text: 'A draft for the next release from the moment the last one ships, with scope, candidates, readiness and a suggested version', status: 'built' as const, href: docs },
  { text: 'Notes, owner and risk written before the release, saved as a commit to glidepath/releases/next.yaml', status: 'built' as const, href: docs },
  { text: 'Three release modes; Hand-flown sign-off on the release PR as a soft gate, with a notification when one ships without it', status: 'built' as const, href: docs },
  { text: 'The record sealed by a pull request to the app\'s gitops repo, with gates, commit proof, check ride and change request frozen in', status: 'built' as const, href: docs },
  { text: 'An outcome after a seven-day bake, written back by a second pull request: clean, rolled back, replaced or stopped', status: 'built' as const, href: docs },
  { text: 'The lifecycle spec behind it, R1 to R4 shipped in Tower v0.67 to v0.70 on October 13 and 14, 2026', status: 'built' as const, href: spec },
  { text: 'Rollout state from the Rollout\'s own facts, in a tracking record on the dev cluster (ADR-0021)', status: 'built' as const, href: gh('glidepath', 'docs/admin/release-state.md') },
  { text: 'Autoland: releases that reach production with no human', status: 'proposal' as const, href: docs },
  { text: 'SLO burn during the bake window, while the production cluster\'s Prometheus is off', status: 'proposal' as const, href: docs },
  { text: 'Any measure of how people use the record, or any user but me', status: 'proposal' as const },
];
---
<Base title={essay.title} description={essay.dek} section="architecture">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="essay">
      <header class="hero">
        <p class="eyebrow"><a href={u('architecture/')}>Architecture</a> · {essay.minutes} min read</p>
        <h1>The release record, and the <em>users</em> I haven't met</h1>
        <p class="lede">{essay.dek}</p>
      </header>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The part of Hangar I'm fondest of right now is a YAML file. Every time one of the Skyport apps ships to
          production, Tower opens a pull request that adds a file like
          <code>releases/flight-api@1.8.0-4f9c2ab.yaml</code> to the app's GitOps repo. It says what changed, who wrote
          the notes, who signed off, how the commit was proven, what the tests found, and, a week later, how it went.
          Months from now I can open it and get the whole story of that release in one place, without a single tool
          having to still be running.
        </p>
        <p>
          I want to show it off. But I also want to be honest about something the feature makes hard to ignore. Nobody
          has used it but me.
        </p>

        <h2>Discovery, with nobody to discover</h2>
        <p>
          The first of my <a href={u('principles/')}>principles</a> is that the platform is a product, and a product is
          shaped by the people who use it. In <a href={u('architecture/why-airframe/')}>Why a platform still needs a
          service catalog</a> I said I'd start any platform with discovery: fifteen or twenty interviews, a couple of
          onboardings shadowed end to end, and features that come out of what people actually said hurt.
        </p>
        <p>
          Hangar doesn't get to do that. It's my lab. The Skyport apps are made up, and I play every part: the
          developer who merges, the release owner who writes the notes, the reviewer who signs off, the person on call
          when it goes wrong, and the auditor who turns up months later asking who approved what. Every feature in the
          release record is built on what I think those people need. That's a lot of assumptions, made as if I
          understood customers I've never met.
        </p>
        <p>
          I don't think the answer is to stop building. A lab is where you can afford to be wrong. But I think the
          honest thing is to treat each feature the way a product team treats an idea before it has data: as a
          hypothesis, written down, with a way to find out if it's wrong. So that's how this essay goes. Here's the
          problem I believe the release record solves, how it works, and then the guesses underneath it, out in the
          open.
        </p>

        <h2>The problem: a release's story is everywhere</h2>
        <p>
          Ask a team what went out last Tuesday and how it went, and the answer usually lives in eight places. The pull
          requests are in the forge. The builds were in the CI system until the runs were pruned. The approval is a
          review on a PR nobody can find. The rollout state sat on a cluster until it was cleaned up. The change ticket
          is in the change tool. The release notes were written the night before from <code>git log</code>, if they
          were written at all. And how it went is in a chat thread, or in someone's head.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each of those is fine on its own. Together they mean a release has no story, only scattered evidence. The
          questions people ask about a release are simple, though, and I think there are about six of them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <Diagram id="releases/02-six-questions-one-file" />

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The left-hand column of that diagram is the first assumption. Those are the people I picture asking, and I've
          never asked them.
        </p>

        <h2>The first version was a receipt</h2>
        <p>
          Tower's first release record was written after the fact. Once a release was healthy in its first Flight
          environment, a poller opened a pull request with everything Tower knew about it, and people could add a
          summary, a risk level and approvals afterwards. When I sat in an app owner's seat for the
          <a href={u('library/tower-field-report-2/')}>second field report</a>, release management was the part of
          Tower I trusted most.
        </p>
        <p>
          But writing the <a href={spec}>lifecycle spec</a> on October 13, 2026, I noticed the record showed up after
          every decision it could have informed. Scope had been gathered, notes written, a build picked and the go or
          no-go made, all before Tower wrote anything down. An approval added once a release is already healthy in
          production isn't really an approval. It's an acknowledgement. And the confidence score handed every release
          five points for "no incidents", because Tower had no way to record one.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the record moved earlier and ended later. The four changes in that spec shipped in Tower v0.67 to v0.70
          over the next two days. It's a small example of the loop I'm describing, with the only user I have: I used
          it, it was wrong in a way I could see, and I changed it.
        </p>

        <h2>How it works</h2>
        <p>
          Follow one release of Skyport's flight-api. The moment its last release, DOGWOOD, reaches Flight, a draft for
          the next one exists. It's called ELM. Releases are named in order per app, the way hurricanes are, because
          "DOGWOOD, shipped as amber-heron" is easier to say in a standup than an image tag.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The draft builds itself.</strong> Its scope is every pull request merged to main since DOGWOOD's commit
          landed, with the issues they close. Open PRs show as incoming, not in scope, because no image contains them
          yet. Every image built from main since then is a candidate, with where it's running on Ground and for how
          long. Readiness checks the leading candidate: merged, running on Ground for 24 hours, built and scanned,
          signed and attested, a passed check ride, a version bump, and a healthy release to roll back to. It suggests a
          version from the kinds of change merged. None of that is stored. Tower computes it from what it already reads.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="still"><a href={still('01-three-cards.png')} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src={still('01-three-cards.png')} alt="The top of flight-api's Releases tab: the next release, ELM, as a draft; DOGWOOD live in prod; and how CEDAR, the one before, turned out. Screens from Hangar's Skyport demo, with invented data." loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>The top of flight-api's Releases tab: the next release, ELM, as a draft; DOGWOOD live in prod; and how CEDAR, the one before, turned out. Screens from Hangar's Skyport demo, with invented data.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure class="still"><a href={still('02-next-release-draft.png')} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src={still('02-next-release-draft.png')} alt="ELM's draft: two PRs in scope, two still open, one candidate build and the readiness checks, all computed. Nothing here is saved." loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>ELM's draft: two PRs in scope, two still open, one candidate build and the readiness checks, all computed. Nothing here is saved.</figcaption></figure>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <strong>People write the rest, early.</strong> The one part a person types is the notes and the owner: a short
          summary, release notes in Keep a Changelog sections, known issues, how to roll back, and a risk level. A button
          fills empty sections from the merged PR titles. Saving makes a direct commit to one file in the app repo,
          which builds nothing:
        </p>
        <pre><code>{draft}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <figure class="still"><a href={still('03-notes-and-owner.png')} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src={still('03-notes-and-owner.png')} alt="Notes and owner, the one part a person writes. Saving commits glidepath/releases/next.yaml and builds nothing." loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>Notes and owner, the one part a person writes. Saving commits glidepath/releases/next.yaml and builds nothing.</figcaption></figure>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <strong>Sign-off moves onto the release PR.</strong> Promote sends the leading candidate through Glidepath's
          normal release path, a pull request to the GitOps repo. What that PR needs depends on the app's release mode.
          Assisted, the default, just records who merged and who reviewed. Hand-flown needs an approving review from one
          of the app's owners or an admin, who isn't the person merging. GitHub's free plan can't require a check on a
          private repo, so this is a soft gate: Tower sets a <code>hangar/release-signoff</code> status on the PR every
          two minutes, and if it merges without a sign-off that counts, a high-severity notification says so and the
          record says so forever. The modes themselves live in a platform-owned file, and changing one is a pull
          request.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The record is sealed in git.</strong> Within a few minutes of the release being healthy in its first
          Flight environment, Tower opens a pull request adding the record to the app's GitOps repo. The draft is copied
          in and cleared for the next release. The evidence is frozen at the same time: every release gate's result,
          how the commit was proven, the check ride the qa gate read, and the change request if the app has one. Those
          sources don't last. Pipeline runs get pruned, and Glidepath sweeps its tracking records after 14 days. The
          record keeps saying what was true when it shipped.
        </p>
        <pre><code>{record}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <figure class="still"><a href={still('04-notes-and-sign-off.png')} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src={still('04-notes-and-sign-off.png')} alt="DOGWOOD's sealed record: the notes written before it shipped, and a sign-off that counted, given on the release PR before the merge." loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>DOGWOOD's sealed record: the notes written before it shipped, and a sign-off that counted, given on the release PR before the merge.</figcaption></figure>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <strong>Then it waits to hear how it went.</strong> The record stays open through a seven-day bake in
          production. Once the outcome is final, a second pull request writes it into the same file: clean, rolled back
          (naming the release it rolled back to and why), replaced before it was healthy, or stopped before production.
          After that, anyone can add what they checked in production, follow-ups and acknowledgements, through a pull
          request to the record. Opt in, and Tower also publishes a GitHub Release named <code>ELM · v1.9.0</code> with
          the notes as its body.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="still"><a href={still('05-outcome.png')} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src={still('05-outcome.png')} alt="CEDAR's outcome, written back once it was final: clean, healthy until DOGWOOD replaced it. The record says plainly that SLO burn isn't measured yet." loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>CEDAR's outcome, written back once it was final: clean, healthy until DOGWOOD replaced it. The record says plainly that SLO burn isn't measured yet.</figcaption></figure>

      <div class="prose">
      </div>

      <Diagram id="releases/01-one-release-in-git" />

      <div class="prose">
        <p class="pull">Git gets every decision. The cluster keeps only what is still moving.</p>

        <h2>Store everything in git</h2>
        <p>
          The second of my principles is that every change is a reviewed commit, and the release record is that idea
          applied to a release's history rather than its config. The draft is a commit. The release mode is a pull
          request. The record is a pull request, and so is its outcome. Even the sign-off is a review on a pull request.
          Tower writes all of them through Backstage's backend and owns none of them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Why go to that trouble when a database would be simpler? Because of who might need it, and when. If Tower were
          deleted tomorrow, every release's story would still be in the repo, readable with nothing more than
          <code>git log</code>. Each record is diffable, so comparing two releases is comparing two files. It sits right
          next to the thing it describes: the GitOps repo that says what's deployed also says how it got there. An
          auditor already knows how to read a git history and trusts it in a way they'd never trust a dashboard. And an
          agent can read a YAML file without learning Tower's API. The readiness score is worked out only from what the
          committed record carries, so a release scores the same in the list, in a comparison and when you open it.
        </p>
        <p>
          It isn't everything, though, and I'd rather draw the line clearly than pretend. A release that's rolling out
          changes every few seconds. That state lives in a tracking record on the dev cluster, fed by the facts each
          Rollout posts (Glidepath's ADR-0021), because nobody should review a pull request every time a canary step
          moves. The draft's scope and candidates are computed and never saved. The bake is read live. The rule I've
          ended up with is that git gets every decision and every final answer, and the cluster keeps what is still
          moving. When it stops moving, Tower writes the end of the story into git.
        </p>
        <p>
          There's a cost. One release can mean a commit and three pull requests, and on a busy team that's a lot of
          noise in a GitOps repo. Tower keeps record PRs out of the release gates, but whether real teams would find
          that history precious or annoying is another thing I don't know.
        </p>

        <h2>What it's trying to fix</h2>
        <p>
          Pulled together, these are the problems I built the release record to solve. Each one comes from releases I've
          lived through in my career, not from Hangar's users, because it hasn't got any.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Notes written the night before.</strong> The draft exists from the moment the last release ships and
          fills itself from merged work, so notes can grow as the release does.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Approvals that come after the fact.</strong> Sign-off sits on the release PR, before the merge, where
          it can still stop something.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Evidence that disappears.</strong> Gate results, commit proof, test evidence and the change request
          are frozen into a file that outlives the systems they came from.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>"Which build was that?"</strong> A release has a name people can say. The image slug still says
          which build went out.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Scores that flatter.</strong> Readiness says how ready a release was when it shipped. The outcome says
          what happened next. They sit side by side and never get folded into one number, and the free "no incidents"
          points are gone.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Releases that end in silence.</strong> Every record gets an outcome, and a rollback says what it went
          back to and why.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Change management without a change board.</strong> An app that goes through change management gets its
          change request number and state on the record, beside a light peer sign-off. External approval boards slow
          delivery without lowering the change failure rate, which is one of the clearer findings in the DORA research,
          so I'd rather have the evidence travel with the release.
        </p>

        <h2>The guesses underneath</h2>
        <p>
          Here's the part I promised. Every one of those fixes rests on something I believe about how teams release
          software. I've written the main ones down, with what I built on each, and the test I'd run if I had a team to
          run it with.
        </p>
      </div>

      <Diagram id="releases/03-built-on-assumptions" />

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Some of the numbers are pure judgment. Twenty-four hours on Ground before readiness is happy. A seven-day bake.
          Autoland eligibility at five recorded outcomes, under 15% rolled back and an average readiness of 80. They're
          sensible-sounding, and they're mine. Some of the design is taste: I like the tree names, and a real team might
          find them twee within a week. And some of it might be aimed at the wrong person entirely. I've assumed the
          release owner is the one who cares most, when it might really be the person on call, who just wants to know
          what changed in the last hour.
        </p>
        <p>
          What I'm fairly sure of is the shape: a release is something with a lifecycle, and a deploy is just one event
          in it. Nearly every release tool I looked at for the spec has landed somewhere near that. Which parts of the
          lifecycle people actually care about is the bit I'd expect to be wrong.
        </p>

        <h2>What I'd do with real users</h2>
        <p>
          If I took this into a company tomorrow, I wouldn't roll it out to everyone. I'd find one team that releases
          often and sit beside them for a release or two, end to end, before showing them anything. Then I'd talk to each
          of the people in that second diagram on their own, the owner, the reviewer, whoever's on call and whoever does
          the audits, and ask what they looked for the last time a release went badly, and where they found it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then I'd measure, because interviews tell you what people think they do. Do notes exist before Promote, and
          does anyone change the pre-fill? How long is it between a sign-off and the merge, and is it ever under a
          minute? Does anyone open a record after its outcome is written? Does the export ever leave the building?
          Tower records none of that today, and it would be the first thing I'd add. Then I'd keep what's used, change
          what's used wrongly, and retire what nobody touches, even the parts I'm fond of.
        </p>
        <p>
          And I'd keep going back. The release record I've built is my best guess at a first version, which means it's
          really a draft of the second one. The second one should be written with someone else.
        </p>

        <h2>What isn't built</h2>
        <p>
          A few things are missing, and they're on the record's own docs page as well. Autoland doesn't exist yet: it
          needs real test results and canary metrics before it could decide anything on its own. The bake doesn't watch
          SLO burn, because the production cluster's Prometheus is off. Tower has never tracked incidents, so an outcome
          can say rolled back but not "caused a page". Outcomes depend on Glidepath's tracking records, so releases older
          than 14 days and releases to cloud targets don't get one. Linked issues are only found through closing keywords
          like <code>Fixes #12</code>. Announcements go to Tower's activity feed, not to chat. The full detail is in
          <a href={docs}>Tower's release record docs</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Why I still like it</h2>
        <p>
          For all the guessing, I keep coming back to that YAML file. Software teams lose so much of their own history,
          and most of it isn't lost through carelessness. It's lost because the story was spread across tools that were
          each built to answer something else. Writing it down in one place, in git, as it happens, feels like a kindness
          to whoever comes next, even if that's just me in six months, wondering why a release was rolled back one Tuesday afternoon.
        </p>
        <p>
          I'd love to find out which of my guesses are wrong. That would mean someone was using it.
        </p>
        <p class="sign">James</p>
        <p class="more"><a href={u('architecture/')}>← All architecture essays</a></p>
      </div>

      <Evidence items={evidence} heading="Where Hangar does this, and where it doesn't yet" />
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
<style>
  .essay .hero .eyebrow a { color: inherit; }
  .essay .hero h1 { max-width: 22ch; }
  .prose h2 { margin-top: 3rem; }
  .prose pre {
    margin: 1.4rem 0; padding: 0.9rem 1rem; overflow-x: auto; border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 6px;
    background: var(--raised); font-size: 0.84rem; line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .prose pre code { background: none; padding: 0; font-size: inherit; }
  .pull {
    font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 1.4vw + 1rem, 2rem); line-height: 1.25;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding: 0.2rem 0 0.2rem 1.2rem; margin: 2.5rem 0; max-width: 34ch;
  }
  .still { margin: 2rem 0; }
  .still img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid var(--rule); background: var(--cockpit); }
  .still figcaption { margin-top: 0.45rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .sign { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.4rem; margin-top: 2rem; }
  .more { margin-top: 2rem; }
</style>
